\section{The one rule}{

A \code{subtext} naming \strong{no} placeholder is simply appended to the default footer, which is what a
note has always done. Writing one placeholder on a line of its own takes the layout over: only
what you name is printed. An unknown \verb{<...>} is not a placeholder --- raw html, \code{"n < 30"} and
\code{"<30 ans>"} pass through verbatim and claim nothing (\verb{\\<} escapes a literal \code{<}).
}

\section{What is built when}{

Every placeholder is resolved \strong{at render}, so a footer follows the \verb{lang =}, the \verb{theme =} and
the medium of the call that prints it, and a table edited after it was built (a \code{select()}, a
\code{\link[=set_display]{set_display()}}, a \code{\link[=set_color_breaks]{set_color_breaks()}}) says the truth about what it now shows. Your own lines
are frozen in the language you wrote them --- the default template holds no prose, so nothing
mixes unless you write it.

A table that has lost its attributes keeps what its \strong{columns} can still say --- the colour
legend and the stars key --- and drops the rest.
